Title: Diagnosis
Details: The failing test `testAddNaN` expects that adding a NaN complex number to another complex produces a Complex.NaN result. The test fails, indicating this behavior does not hold in the current add method.

From the code snippet of `Complex.add(Complex rhs)`:
```java
public Complex add(Complex rhs) throws NullArgumentException {
    MathUtils.checkNotNull(rhs);
    return createComplex(real + rhs.getReal(),
        imaginary + rhs.getImaginary());
}
```
we see there is no explicit check for NaN values in "this" or rhs. The method directly adds the real and imaginary parts and returns a new Complex from those sums.

Looking at the constructor of Complex:
```java
public Complex(double real, double imaginary) {
    super();
    this.real = real;
    this.imaginary = imaginary;

    isNaN = Double.isNaN(real) || Double.isNaN(imaginary);
    isInfinite = !isNaN &&
    (Double.isInfinite(real) || Double.isInfinite(imaginary));
}
```
`isNaN` flag is set if either part is NaN. However, if the add method adds a finite number to Double.NaN, according to Java Double arithmetic rules, the result is NaN, so one might think the new Complex would have NaN parts. But the problem is that the sum `real + rhs.getReal()` for example, if rhs is Complex.NaN, would be NaN plus some number yielding NaN. So the new Complex will be constructed with NaN parts and should reflect isNaN = true.

But the test shows this is not happening, indicating `Complex.NaN` might be a special constant instance representing a NaN Complex. The add method returns a new Complex object; it doesn't return this special NaN instance explicitly. 

In contrast, the `subtract(Complex rhs)` method checks explicitly:
```java
if (isNaN || rhs.isNaN) {
    return NaN;
}
```
This check returns the canonical NaN constant instance if either operand is NaN, so behavior is consistent with javadoc stating NaN is returned if either operand has any NaN part.

The inconsistency is that the `add` method does not perform this NaN check and does not return Complex.NaN explicitly, whereas subtract does. Also, the javadocs for both say:

```
If either this or rhs has a NaN value in either part, {@link #NaN} is returned;
otherwise Infinite and NaN values are returned in the parts of the result according to the rules for java.lang.Double arithmetic
```

The javadoc agree on returning the canonical NaN constant instance on NaN input, but add violates this contract.

Hence, the root cause of the bug is that the `add` method lacks the NaN check and does not return `Complex.NaN` when either operand is NaN, unlike the subtract method.

This results in failing test `testAddNaN` which expects `z.isNaN()` to be true because `z` should be the canonical Complex.NaN instance, but it is not.

Recent bug reports are irrelevant here.

# Summary:
- `add` does not check for NaN operands and return `Complex.NaN`.
- `subtract` properly checks and returns `Complex.NaN`.
- Both javadocs declare same contract.
- Test expects that contract for add to be fulfilled, but it isn't.

Fix should add NaN check in `add` method similar to subtract.

Hence, the bug is caused by missing isNaN check and direct creation of new Complex in add, violating the NaN contract and causing test failure.

